David Damerell <damerell@chiark.greenend.org.uk> writes:

> I'm confused now. The main loop is actually traversed once per player
> input. If the last player input didn't take any game time (eg #adjust) it
> just hops down and gets some more input. Otherwise it subtracts 12 from
> the player's accumulated movement points to account for the cost of the
> action just taken. Then it alternates monster moves and player moves
> (monsters first) until no-one has more than 12 movement points left. Then
> it chalks up a new turn, and gives each monster movement points equal to
> its speed, and the player 12 at normal speed, 12 or 18 if fast, 18 or 24
> if very fast, reduced for encumbrance; then it checks to see if various
> timeouts have happened (I think this is where you die); then finally it
> checks to see if the player has 12 or more movement points. If so, it gets
> more input; if not, it jumps back to doing some monster moves (which don't
> alternate with player moves because the player doesn't have more than 12
> movement points).

I'm not sure if I fully understand this (and maybe I'd do better if
you pointed me at the relevant part of the code).  Here's my attempt
at writing out a simplified step-by-step version, ignoring player
inputs that don't take any game time, and that last "if not, it jumps
back to doing some monster moves" which will only happen if you're
encumbered.

1. It gets some player input, does the appropriate action, and
   subtracts 12 of the player's movement points.

2. It alternates monster moves and player moves until everyone is at
   12 or fewer movement points.  Questions:
    * If someone has exactly 12 movement points during this step, do
      they get to move?  (I'll assume no.)
    * If a monster has a lot more movement points than the player,
      will it get multiple consecutive moves here?  (I'll assume yes.)

3. It advances the turn counter and doles out movement points to
   everyone.

4. It checks the timeouts - if you are sick, it reduces your ttl by
   one, and if it is zero or less, you die.

5. It goes back to step 1.

> So I don't think you can have _no_ chance to act unless you are
> encumbered.

What I meant was that it would make things too easy if the order of
events was always:

Demogorgon stings.
You waste a move on something other than healing.
Timeouts - you die.

And never just:

Demogorgon stings.
Timeouts - you die.

> I do wonder what the significance of "12 or more" versus "more than 12" is
> here - can we produce some movement oddities? Probably only around the
> turn we go from normal speed to Very Fast.

It may depend on the answers to my questions above, but as I said,
unless you're encumbered the "if you have 12 or more" condition will
always be true, because you've just received at least 12 movement
points.

